


Jonah gave a short laugh. “You can call anything possible.”




“Until we know more, yes.”




A heavy contact sounded from the shelter’s direction.




They turned together.




The main camera captured only the open structure and the equipment on the table. The emitter’s green indicator remained visible. The generator continued behind the rear wall.




Jonah moved toward the shelter.




Maren caught his sleeve. “Wait.”




“That was inside.”




“We’re both outside. Keep the camera here.”




“So we stand here while it does it again?”




“Yes.”




They waited in the clearing. The insects continued around them. Water moved beyond the trees. The branch touched the roof once, lightly enough that Maren almost missed it.




No second impact came.




After a minute, she released Jonah’s sleeve.




He looked at her hand before she pulled it back. “You stopped me for that one.”




“Yes.”




He let out a slow breath.




“I’m asking you not to talk like it didn’t happen just because we don’t know what caused it,” he said.




Maren returned to the table. She checked the camera and recorder displays. The heavy contact appeared on both shelter microphones and weakly on the trail recorder. The main camera had shifted exposure when they moved outside, but the frame remained stable. Nothing visible crossed it.




She marked the event as an impact inside or adjacent to the shelter, source unknown.




Jonah watched her write. “Are we done?”




“We should be.”




“You said one session.”




“We completed one.”




“And we got the sound.”




Maren said nothing.




Jonah watched her.




Maren looked at the clock. They had more than four hours before pickup and less than one hour before the first scheduled check-in. She should shut down, pack the emitter, and use the remaining hours only to leave. They had captured the call and logged their positions. Every additional question would weaken the record they already had.




She looked toward the dark beyond the trail camera.




“Turn the emitter off,” she said.




Jonah did not reach for it. “Then what?”




“We check the trail and river side from the marked route. Ten minutes out, ten back. We do not follow anything off the path.”




“So you want to check that side.”




“I want to check what’s there before we leave.”




He lowered the toggle.




The electronic whine stopped.




The generator still ran. The crickets cut off almost together. The scattered clicks from the low ground stopped a moment later. Water and engine noise remained, stripped of the smaller sounds that had filled the spaces between them.




Maren counted.




One.




Two.




Three.




Four.




A single cricket began near the trail. Others answered at uneven distances until the clearing returned to its earlier noise.




By then, she had already lifted the camera.




Scene 5 — Wrong History




By the time Maren reached the first trail marker, the shelter had disappeared behind the trees.




She stopped and turned the camera back. Jonah’s headlamp moved between the trunks thirty feet behind her. Beyond him, the generator’s work light showed a dull patch against the rear wall, but the roof and stone pillars had blended into the wooded slope. The engine reached her as a low pulse. Between pulses of the motor, she could hear water, insects, and distant traffic.




“Position,” Jonah called.




Maren aimed the camera at the blue triangle on the post. “Blue route below the shelter. Eight seventeen. Emitter off. Generator remains active at the shelter.”




Jonah joined her with the handheld recorder. He had left the audio case, fixed camera, and other recorders running. The route sheet was folded into his back pocket. He pointed down the trail.




“The call came from that side.”




“Show me.”




Jonah started down the trail.




Maren followed with the camera.




Neither of them spoke for the first few steps.




The trail descended through cottonwoods and young ash. Their lights reached the next curve but not the ground beyond it. Loose stone shifted under their boots. As the generator faded behind them, the river sounded louder. At one bend Maren heard it ahead of her. Twenty steps later she heard it from behind her through the trees. A vehicle crossed the bridge upstream, its engine arriving in two separate swells divided by the rise.




Maren filmed the roadward gap in the canopy and then the slope below the trail. “Multiple sound paths,” she said. “River corridor, bridge, county road, and shelter generator all audible from the same position.”




Jonah held the recorder toward the low ground. “None of them sounds like what we heard.”




“That’s why we’re checking.”




They continued. The blue markers ended at a post where a white triangle pointed right along the river loop. A narrow service path crossed to the left, barred by a brown sign that read PRESERVE STAFF ONLY. The marked route continued to the right.




Maren filmed the sign before turning. Jonah stood at the junction and looked left long enough that she stopped moving.




“Marked route,” she said.




“I know.”




He followed her to the right.




The river loop narrowed between the bank and a wooded rise. Quartzite broke through the soil in low red shelves. Water showed between trunks as brief black planes reflecting pieces of their lights. Frogs called from farther downstream, a few low notes followed by a rough cluster from the opposite bank. Crickets continued above them on the dry slope. The two groups started and stopped independently.




Maren lowered the camera to film the trail surface. Deer tracks crossed the damp soil near the river. Raccoon prints followed the edge for several feet before disappearing beneath leaves. A broken branch leaned over the path at shoulder height, its exposed wood pale against the bark.




Jonah moved ahead until the beam from his headlamp reached the next marker.




“Stay in frame,” Maren said.




He slowed without looking back.




The generator faded. Its vibration no longer entered the ground, and the ringing in Maren’s right ear became more noticeable in the quieter air. She swallowed twice. The pressure shifted but did not clear completely.




A long animal call rose across the river.




Jonah stopped.




This sound did not resemble the cry. It began with several short barks, climbed into a wavering yowl, and divided into quick pitches that overlapped as echoes returned from the corridor. Two more calls followed from farther away. For several seconds they sounded like a larger group moving along the opposite bank.




“Coyotes,” Maren said.




Jonah kept the recorder raised. “I know.”




The calls broke into shorter notes and ended. Frogs resumed near the water.




Maren filmed the opposite bank though the camera could show only trunks, brush, and the near edge of the river. “Coyote group across or beyond the river. Direction uncertain because of echo.”




Jonah walked on.




The trail curved away from the water and climbed. The air warmed during the first part of the ascent. Sweat cooled beneath Maren’s collar when the slope leveled, and then a colder current crossed the path from the left. It moved close to the ground. Her shins felt it first. The temperature change reached her hands a second later and ended before she had taken six steps.
